==== OEM easy installation ====

**Note:** Reset router to factory defaults if it has been previously configured. Installing OpenWrt on this device requires a two-step process, first writing the initramfs image from the OEM firmware - and then finalizing the installation by sysupgrading the real device firmware.

  - Enable ssh in the OEM firmware (use port 22).
  - Copy openwrt-ipq40xx-generic-asus_map-ac2200-initramfs-fit-uImage.itb via scp to /tmp/
  - ''mtd-write -d linux -i /tmp/openwrt-ipq40xx-generic-asus_map-ac2200-initramfs-fit-uImage.itb''
  - ''reboot -f''
  - Now use the Ethernet port right beside the power connector.
  - When booting, the LED will //breathe// in multiple colors, then blink blue, and when ready, will have a steady blue color.
  - Wait for it to reboot. It will now have the IP 192.168.1.1, and the login will be ''root'' without a password.
  - Copy openwrt-ipq40xx-generic-asus_map-ac2200-squashfs-sysupgrade.bin via scp to /tmp/
  - ''sysupgrade -n /tmp/openwrt-ipq40xx-generic-asus_map-ac2200-squashfs-sysupgrade.bin''
  - Wait for it to reboot
  - ssh to 192.168.1.1 and set a root password, or browse to ''<nowiki>http://192.168.1.1</nowiki>'' if LuCI is installed.


==== OEM installation using the TFTP method ====

->  [[docs:guide-user:installation:generic.flashing.tftp]]

The Asus MAP-AC2200 doesn't have a reliable push-button tftp recovery, as it can't deal with changed UBI partitioning (OEM and OpenWrt differ in this regard). Recovering the MAP-AC2200 requires opening the device and attaching a serial console to the unpopulated header "J35" (2mm spacing), the pinout is: 3.3v - RX - TX - GND (square is VCC).

Prepare your tftp server (192.168.1.70) and provide "asus_map-ac2200-initramfs-fit-uImage.itb" as "HIVESPOT.trx" to the MAP-AC2200.

Interrupt u-boot at the boot screen, pressing "1", if you followed the default settings below, you can keep the defaults (press [ENTER]):
<code>
Please choose the operation: 
   1: Load System code to SDRAM via TFTP.
   2: Load System code then write to Flash via TFTP.
   3: Boot System code via Flash (default).
   4: Entr boot command line interface.
   7: Load Boot Loader code then write to Flash via Serial.
   9: Load Boot Loader code then write to Flash via TFTP.


You choosed 1


   1: Load System code to SDRAM via TFTP.
 Please Input new ones /or Ctrl-C to discard
	Input device IP (192.168.1.1) ==:

	Input server IP (192.168.1.70) ==:

	Input Linux Kernel filename (HIVESPOT.trx) ==:
</code>

Once the initramfs image has booted, sysupgrade can be used to install a known-working sysupgrade image of OpenWrt from the login shell provided on the serial console.

=== Specific values needed for tftp ===

^ Bootloader tftp client IPv4 address  | 192.168.1.1   |
^ Firmware tftp image                  | [[:downloads|Latest OpenWrt release]] (**''NOTE:''** Name must contain //"asus_map-ac2200-initramfs-fit-uImage.itb"//) |
^ TFTP window start                    | select //"1: Load System code to SDRAM via TFTP.//" manually over serial  |
^ TFTP client required IP address      | 192.168.1.70                                        |


